:"harmful","confidence":90,"evidenceIds":["E12","E2"]},{"itemId":"I23","answer":"sufficient","confidence":88,"evidenceIds":["E8","E14","E15","E12"]}],"evidenceRequests":["Q3","Q2","Q1"],"summary":"The on-time decline is real: AVL is validated by manual ride checks, and ridership and dwell time are flat. The drivers are the April recovery-time cuts and the Halvorsen and Bridgeport construction. Group C, with neither, is unchanged. Group B, with cuts only, fell about 10 points. Group A, with both, fell about 31 points. The 40% absence rise is mostly a recording artifact from ShiftLine, which began logging partial-day absences. Full-day absences rose only 1.1%. The independent dispatch log shows no change in unoperated trips or late pull-outs. The attendance program would punish operators for a recording change, so it is harmful. Restore recovery time, set construction-period running times, and restate the absence trend by type."}}